WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.550 --> 00:00:05.230
In the Friedel-Crafts acylation reaction an

00:00:05.569 --> 00:00:10.839
aromatic compound is reacted with an acid chloride in the presence of aluminum trichloride.

00:00:11.930 --> 00:00:15.129
This adds the acyl group onto the aromatic ring.

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:18.729
Let's look at the mechanism of this reaction. In

00:00:19.369 --> 00:00:23.199
the first step, the acid chloride is going to react with aluminum trichloride.

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:30.790
The chlorine will be transferred onto aluminum in a process similar to Friedel-Crafts alkylation.

00:00:30.919 --> 00:00:33.609
You may want to check out my video on that if you haven't already.

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:37.659
So, the chlorine is going to go with its lone pairs

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:40.280
onto aluminum.

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:46.420
This gives us a negatively charged aluminum complex, and a positively charged a acylium ion.

00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:51.819
The acylium Ion also has a resonance form that puts the positive charge on oxygen.

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:59.650
This is an important contributing structure because we have an octet on oxygen and on carbon in this form. Now,

00:01:00.290 --> 00:01:05.949
we can show the double bond of benzene attacking at the carbon of our acylium ion

00:01:06.409 --> 00:01:08.798
pushing the electrons on to oxygen.

00:01:09.829 --> 00:01:13.178
Now to get to our final product all that's left is a deprotonation,

00:01:15.079 --> 00:01:19.898
although you'll sometimes see this shown as a spontaneous process with loss of H+.

00:01:20.329 --> 00:01:23.619
Probably the best way to draw this is to show a chlorine

00:01:24.319 --> 00:01:26.349
dissociating from the aluminum complex,

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:29.890
deprotonating,

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:35.199
making the compound aromatic and producing a molecule of HCl.

00:01:36.710 --> 00:01:44.019
Let's look at resonance in the product that we just made so that we can better understand the nature of an acylated benzene derivative.

00:01:44.929 --> 00:01:50.199
Notice that we have these conjugated double bonds here so we can push electrons outside

00:01:50.539 --> 00:01:53.019
of the ring through the oxygen atom.

00:01:57.979 --> 00:02:05.169
This charge separated resonance form isn't the best resonance form for this molecule, but it does contribute to the overall structure.

00:02:05.719 --> 00:02:07.928
Let's continue to push electrons around the ring.

00:02:10.489 --> 00:02:17.619
Now our carbocation is para to this group and we can push electrons once again to show a final resonance form.

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:27.310
There are two things that we should notice. First of all, all the resonance forms put positive charges on the ring

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:29.150
this

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:32.200
deactivates the ring toward further electrophilic aromatic substitution.

00:02:33.130 --> 00:02:33.709
If

00:02:33.709 --> 00:02:39.669
this already has positive character, it doesn't really want to attack a positive electrophile again.

00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:42.249
So, this ring is deactivated

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:44.480
toward further reaction.

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:51.580
We should also notice that the positive charge in the resonance forms is concentrated on the ortho and para positions.

00:02:52.070 --> 00:02:58.839
This means when it does react with an electrophile in EAS, it will do so at the meta

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:01.010
positions.

00:03:01.010 --> 00:03:08.620
Doing the Friedel-Crafts acylation reaction solves a lot of problems that you would have if you did the Friedel-Crafts alkylation reaction.

00:03:09.350 --> 00:03:13.989
But before I talk to you about that I first need to show you a new reaction. A

00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:19.120
carbonyl group can actually be reduced to a CH2 group.

00:03:19.670 --> 00:03:25.839
These methods are both named in honor of their discoverers: The first is the Wolff- Kishner reaction.

00:03:26.239 --> 00:03:30.849
By treating the carbonyl compound with hydrazine in the presence of NaOH

00:03:31.220 --> 00:03:34.600
We can reduce it all the way down to a CH2 group.

00:03:35.390 --> 00:03:42.250
The second way to do this is the Clemmensen reduction, which uses zinc/mercury amalgam in the presence of acid.

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:45.939
Okay, now let's get back to the problems with the Friedel-Crafts

00:03:46.519 --> 00:03:53.349
alkylation reaction and see why acylation can be used to solve them them. The alkyl group is a weakly

00:03:53.690 --> 00:03:55.160
activating group and

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:57.999
stabilizes the formation of negative charge on the ring,

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:05.529
whereas the acyl group has the resonance we saw above, so it deactivates the ring to further attack.

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:09.070
Recall with the Friedel-Crafts alkylation reaction

00:04:09.829 --> 00:04:14.289
we saw this substrate reacting to add on two alkyl groups

00:04:14.329 --> 00:04:21.279
and we couldn't stop with just one. Well, because this product pulls electron density out of the ring

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:27.640
It's not likely to react further so it really won't want to react twice with our acyl chloride.

00:04:28.010 --> 00:04:29.900
Since the ring is deactivated,

00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:34.030
further EAS reactions that are unwanted are less of a problem.

00:04:34.790 --> 00:04:38.619
Remember carbocation rearrangements were a problem with Friedel-Crafts

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:46.630
alkylation? Even if we wanted to make a straight chain on here of carbon atoms,  we couldn't because of carbocation rearrangements.

00:04:47.570 --> 00:04:51.789
The compound would rearrange to form a more stable carbocation.

00:04:54.470 --> 00:05:01.539
If instead we acylate the ring with a three- carbon acid chloride we can get a linear product.

00:05:02.510 --> 00:05:07.689
Now we can apply one of the reductions that we just learned to make it into the straight chained

00:05:08.389 --> 00:05:13.029
alkyl compound. So, in a couple steps, we can get to straight chain

00:05:13.370 --> 00:05:18.220
alkylated compounds that weren't possible to make with the Friedel-Crafts alkylation reaction.

00:05:19.190 --> 00:05:26.109
The third issues circumvented by this new reaction—the acylation reaction—is the problem with deactivated rings.

00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:28.539
When there's an electron withdrawing group

00:05:29.240 --> 00:05:35.829
Friedel-Crafts alkylation will not work. The carbocation is simply not a powerful enough

00:05:36.620 --> 00:05:43.389
electrophile to make a deactivated ring push out its electrons and attack it. However, when we use

00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:50.619
Friedel-Crafts acylation conditions, we generate the acylium ion, which is a much more powerful

00:05:51.229 --> 00:05:52.550
electrophile, so

00:05:52.550 --> 00:05:57.130
we can perform the Friedel-Crafts acylation on our deactivated rings,

00:05:57.130 --> 00:06:03.729
and then go ahead and reduce them down to alkyl compounds using the Wolff Kishner reaction or Clemmensen reduction

00:06:04.039 --> 00:06:06.039
If we need to. In

00:06:06.500 --> 00:06:12.339
this lesson we reviewed Friedel-Crafts acylation, which puts an acyl group onto an aromatic ring.

00:06:13.130 --> 00:06:18.070
We went over the mechanism and saw how the acylium ion can be generated.

00:06:18.919 --> 00:06:25.839
We looked at resonance in the acylated products and saw that this deactivates the ring and makes it a meta director.

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:31.119
We then looked at how we could overcome problems present in Friedel-Crafts

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:34.599
alkylation by using the acylation reaction.

00:06:34.820 --> 00:06:40.269
KP here. If you learn something, give me a thumbs up on the way out and for more chemistry subscribe to my channel.

